{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# The completion verifier cannot bound a runaway: repetition is detected but nothing acts on it\n\n## The defect\n\nThe completion-verify agent step has **no bound on a runaway**. When the agent falls into a\nrepeating tool-call cycle it burns its entire step budget on redundant calls and is then **killed\nwith no verdict at all** — leaving `--force-close`, the unattested close this project forbids, as\nthe only exit. **20 of 205 epics in this store already carry a `FORCE_CLOSE` audit comment**,\nincluding `1d96` (21 descendants) and `bf50` (19), so this is the established escape hatch, not a\nhypothetical.\n\nThe step budget exists to bound exactly this failure. It does not: it is a flat ceiling that stops\nthe run *after* the waste, rather than a guard that detects the runaway and makes the agent land.\n\n### Measured: three of four real runs of this gate LOOP\n\nFour instrumented live runs, same codebase state except bf31's which predates its own fix:\n\n| run | criteria | calls | **distinct** | redundant | distinct ratio | outcome | `w24 distinct<=0.5` fires |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FULL | 14 | 258 | **238** | 20 | **0.922** | died empty | **never** |\n| SPLIT-A | 7 | 257 | **76** | **181** | 0.296 | died empty | **call 88** |\n| SPLIT-B | 7 | 270 | 167 | 103 | 0.619 | died empty | **call 196** |\n| bf31 pre-fix | 14 | 264 | 135 | 129 | 0.511 | died empty | **call 151** |\n\nRepetition wasted **40-70% of every pathological run**. SPLIT-A is textbook: the IDENTICAL call\n`search_files(\"_loader\", \"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler\")` repeated **144 times**, returning the\nidentical 12,554-char result each time, from call #81 to the end. SPLIT-B looped 62x on\n`search_files(\"deleted\", tests/.../test_backend_characterization.py)` -> an **honest** `(no matches)`\nthe agent simply would not accept. Only the clean FULL run was genuine breadth.\n\n### The mechanism that turns a runaway into \"no verdict\"\n\n1. `build_agent_request` (`src/rebar/llm/workflow/runs.py:327-339`) builds the `RunRequest` for every\n   workflow agent step and never sets `tool_step_limit`; the v3 step schema is closed, so\n   `completion-verification.yaml`'s `decide@then/verify` step has no way to supply one.\n2. The shipped forced-landing primitive is therefore dormant for the one step that needs it:\n   `src/rebar/llm/agent_call.py:64-73` rewrites the toolsets to\n   `.filtered(lambda ctx, _: ctx.run_step <= limit)`. Its only production setter is the bounded\n   recovery sub-call (`completion_recovery.py:471`, `_EVIDENCE_TOOL_STEPS=16`).\n3. Exhaustion raises pydantic-ai `UsageLimitExceeded` -> `interpret_failure`\n   (`structured_run.py:75-101`) raises `LLMRunnerError` -> `CompletionAgentStep.run`\n   (`completion_recovery.py:429-432`) catches only `UnretryableOutputError`, of which\n   `LLMRunnerError` is a strict **ancestor** (`errors.py:29-46`) -> `failure_diagnostic` stays\n   `None` -> `raise_completion_workflow_failure` raises a bare `LLMError` at\n   `completion_recovery.py:400`. No verdict, no `gate_error_v1` sidecar, computed diagnostic\n   discarded one frame from the surface.\n\n---\n\n## WITHDRAWN PLAN (kept visible deliberately — the wrong version is the instructive artifact)\n\n> **An earlier revision of this ticket proposed a FLAT FORCED LANDING: set\n> `tool_step_limit = max(0, request_limit - 1)` (= 239 at the default budget) unconditionally on the\n> primary verify step, so tools are withdrawn near the end of the budget and the agent must emit a\n> verdict.**\n>\n> **WHY IT WAS WITHDRAWN.** The design intent of the step budget is to bound **FAILURES** — runaway\n> tool calls under error conditions — and **NOT to cap capacity**; it was explicitly never meant to\n> restrict tool calls for large epics. A flat landing inverts that: it caps capacity for every run,\n> including healthy ones.\n>\n> The data refutes its necessity as well as its intent. The only run that was genuinely budget-bound\n> by *breadth* is the clean FULL run — 92% distinct signatures, 93.8-100% novelty in its final block.\n> Truncating that at 239 requests would land a verdict on deliberately-truncated evidence for\n> legitimate work: **a worse verdict, not a safer one**, and on a gate that SIGNS its output.\n>\n> The withdrawn plan was not wrong about the mechanism — links 1-3 above still hold, and the\n> ACTUATOR it named (withdraw tools so the agent lands) is retained. It was wrong about the\n> **trigger**: budget-proximity instead of runaway-detection.\n\n---\n\n## THE FIX: trigger the existing actuator on repetition, not on budget proximity\n\nWithdraw tools **when a runaway is detected**, so the agent lands a verdict on the evidence it has.\nHealthy breadth is never touched.\n\n### Blocker: the existing detector's headline metric is INVERTED on real data\n\n`max_consecutive_repeat` (`src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py:213-235`) counts only *adjacent identical*\nsignatures, so it is blind to any cycle of length >= 2:\n\n| trace | what it is | **`max_consecutive_repeat`** |\n|---|---|---|\n| bf31 pre-fix | a genuine **4-call cycle** repeated 31-37x | **1** |\n| FULL | **healthy** breadth | **5** |\n\n**The pathological run scores 1; the healthy run scores 5.** An actuator wired to this metric would\nfire on healthy work and miss real cycles — the exact inversion of what is wanted. It catches\nSPLIT-A's 144x 1-cycle (137) only because the degenerate 1-cycle is the single shape it can see.\nThis is also why [rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a] measured `max_consecutive_repeat=1` and concluded *\"It\nis NOT a runaway loop.\"*\n\n**This ticket therefore `depends_on` [rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0]**, which fixes the metric. Building\nthe actuator against today's signal is the one thing this evidence forbids. Sequencing is not\noptional here.\n\n### The discriminator that works: a windowed distinct-ratio\n\nLowest distinct-ratio over any trailing window, all four runs:\n\n| window | bf31 | SPLIT-A | SPLIT-B | **FULL (healthy)** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 16 | 0.250 | **0.062** | 0.062 | **0.750** |\n| 24 | 0.167 | **0.042** | — | **0.833** |\n| 32 | 0.125 | **0.031** | — | **0.875** |\n\n`distinct(last 24 tool calls) <= 0.50` fires on **all three** pathological runs (calls 151, 88, 196)\nand **never** on the healthy one — a ~5x margin. It is a set cardinality over a window, therefore\n**order-insensitive**, so unlike adjacency it is robust to tool calls completing out of emission\norder (which these traces do).\n\n### Feasibility — confirmed; no new detector is needed\n\n* `run_ctx.messages` **is** available inside the tool-filter predicate (verified against\n  `pydantic_ai.tools.RunContext` fields), alongside `run_ctx.usage.tool_calls`.\n* `usage_log.failure_usage` duck-types on a plain message list (`type(m).__name__ == \"ModelResponse\"`\n  / `\"ToolCallPart\"`), so the existing signature logic runs **verbatim** on the live history.\n* The actuator is already shipped: withdraw tools via `agent_call.py:64-73`.\n\n### Both kill paths must be bound — as FAILURE conditions, not workload ceilings\n\n`build_usage_limits` (`structured_run.py:399-412`) installs two independent ceilings:\n\n    request_limit    = max(1, ceil(eff_max_iter / 2))   # 240 at the default max_iterations=480\n    tool_calls_limit = max(8, eff_max_iter)              # 480\n\n`run_step` increments once per **model request** (`pydantic_ai/_agent_graph.py:856`) and therefore\nbounds only the first. Measured sweep: at 3 tool calls/request a `tool_step_limit` of 239, 216 **and**\n180 all still die. The clean FULL run sat at 258/240 = **1.075**; [rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a]\nmeasured a real run at 475/240 = **1.979**, clearing the 480 ceiling by **2 calls**. A guard keyed on\n`run_step` alone passes a 1-call-per-request test and still dies in production.\n\nBecause the discriminator is computed over **tool calls**, a runaway that burns calls without\nburning requests trips the same signal — so both paths are bound without introducing any workload\nceiling.\n\n**Enforcement point (pinned from the vendored source):** `UsageLimits.check_before_tool_call`\n(`pydantic_ai/usage.py:412-419`) is called from `_agent_graph.py:1703-1706` with **projected** usage\n(`usage.tool_calls + len(calls_to_run)`) and raises **before** executing, so `usage.tool_calls` never\novershoots — the run dies instead. The guard must therefore act while headroom still remains.\n\n---\n\n## HONEST LIMITS OF THIS FIX — do not soften these\n\n**1. This does NOT make e369's full-criteria close reach a verdict.** The clean FULL run never trips\nthe discriminator, so it would still die budget-exhausted on genuine breadth. **Per the stated design\nintent that is CORRECT behaviour, not a bug to route around** — the budget bounds failures, and that\nrun contains no failure to bound.\n\n**2. The path to e369 reaching a verdict is AC-split PLUS this actuator, is two tickets, and promises\nno PASS.** Each half's genuine work (76 and 167 distinct calls) sits inside the budget once its loop\nis cut — but \"reaches a verdict\" is not \"passes\". Criterion #1's Gerrit half remains unreachable by\nany repository tool ([rebar:6ec8-24c7-182b-40d0]), and the flat budget still does not scale with\nclaim volume ([rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a], [rebar:d59e-e042-2b94-4b70]).\n\n## The AC-split fallback: tested live, NOT sufficient — belongs in its own ticket\n\nSplit by explicit AC index (never inferred), each half carrying the full context: 14 criteria ->\n7 + 7, context 99,347 -> 96,631 (A) / 96,086 (B) chars. The criteria partition cleanly (each is an\nindependent `- [x]` item), but **both halves still died empty**, and the workload benefit is unproven:\n\n| run | criteria | distinct calls | **distinct per criterion** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| FULL | 14 | 238 | 17.0 |\n| SPLIT-A | 7 | 76 | 10.9 |\n| SPLIT-B | 7 | 167 | **23.9** |\n\nSPLIT-B spent **more** evidence per criterion than the full run; run-to-run variance (10.9-23.9)\ndominates any halving effect. Two signatures over two disjoint criteria subsets is also a **material\nchange to what the attestation means** and needs its own design. It is a capability, not this bug's\nfix — and only viable at all *with* this actuator, since both halves were killed by loops, not\nbreadth.\n\n## Measurement gap found along the way\n\nThe control-group question (\"how many requests did the epics that PASSED consume?\") **could not be\nanswered honestly**: `usage_log.record()` no-ops unless `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` is set (unset by default),\nand even then `_FIELDS` (`usage_log.py:32`) omits `tool_calls`. Filed as\n[rebar:d166-036c-238c-4b39] — a gate whose spend cannot be measured after the fact cannot be tuned,\nthe same \"computed then discarded\" shape as [rebar:8b97-a0ca-82a4-4d4e].\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] a runaway (a repeating tool-call cycle) is DETECTED during the run and the already-shipped\n      tool-withdrawal actuator is triggered, so the agent lands a `completion_verdict` on the\n      evidence gathered instead of being killed with nothing\n- [ ] the trigger is **runaway detection, not budget proximity** — a run that is doing genuine,\n      high-novelty work is never cut, and this is pinned by a test built from the FULL run's profile\n      (>= 0.9 distinct ratio) asserting the guard does NOT fire\n- [ ] the detector used separates a **k-cycle with k >= 2** from genuine breadth (this is why the\n      ticket depends on [rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0]); a test replays a 4-cycle and asserts the guard\n      fires\n- [ ] **both** kill paths are bound as failure conditions: a runaway that burns `tool_calls` without\n      burning `requests` is caught. The RED test **varies the calls-per-request ratio** and includes\n      a case at ~2-3 calls/request that a `run_step`-only guard fails to bound — a fixture pinned at\n      1 call/request would be vacuous\n- [ ] no new workload ceiling is introduced: no run is cut merely for being large\n- [ ] `tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py:132`\n      (`assert runner.requests[-1].tool_step_limit is None`) still passes UNEDITED — it pins the\n      tool-free finalizer, which this change must not touch\n- [ ] the full CI selection (`pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal`) is\n      green apart from the known pre-existing failure [rebar:354d-c85d-6689-4302]\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest tests/unit/test_agent_call_runaway_guard_c827.py -q\n    pytest tests/unit/test_runner_pydantic_budget.py tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py \\\n           tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py -q\n    pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal -q\n\nEvidence: `/tmp/c827-trace.jsonl` (FULL, 258 rows), `/tmp/c827-split-A.jsonl` (257),\n`/tmp/c827-split-B.jsonl` (270), `/tmp/bf31-trace-evidence.jsonl` (264), with run logs beside them."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785945332248136001,"uuid":"10e0280c-db54-459d-ac2c-c3ee2d9283fd"}